{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"A\": \"Develop a conservative copy/allocation-reduction candidate. Pass immutable graphs, matrices, color arrays and read-only vector arguments by const reference; leave mutable DFS-history changes to B. Replace the linear search for the last partition with move-from-back/pop_back, preserving LIFO processing and child insertion order. Reserve predictable capacities and reuse scratch storage where initialization semantics remain identical. Avoid dangling references across partition removal or vector growth. Keep algorithms, comparisons, traversal order and output unchanged so this candidate provides a low-risk integration base.\",\n  \"B\": \"Optimize path evaluation independently. Build tree adjacency once per spanning tree by traversing stored edges in their existing order and appending both endpoints; this reproduces buscaAresta ordering without repeated full-edge scans. Replace copied histories and linear membership searches with push/pop backtracking and visited state. Maintain color frequencies and distinct-color count incrementally; use storage bounded by actual input colors rather than assuming K is small, while retaining the original nCores stopping criterion. Preserve ascending start vertices, neighbor order, leaf-only evaluation, first tropical-path termination and strict-greater incumbent updates. Do not return an interior path merely because it already contains every color. Preserve behavior on isolated vertices and unsuccessful searches.\",\n  \"C\": \"Research a structurally cheaper, behavior-equivalent Prim_ST. First prove that its committed directed mat_cycle edges form a single-parent forest and each temporary edge targets an unselected vertex; use that invariant to eliminate redundant cycle-matrix construction and cycle searches. Replace copied new_mat with immutable graph data plus symmetric exclusion membership. Compare the simple dense selector against an ordered frontier exploiting unit edge weights, retaining a general fallback where appropriate. Preserve smallest-index selection ties, ascending neighbor processing, first parent assignment, included-edge update order and sentinel behavior, disconnected returns, and spanning-edge emission by vertex index. Do not substitute conventional constrained MST/DSU semantics or silently repair unusual partition predicates: those transformations can change the reference path.\",\n  \"G\": \"Integrate only correctness-validated candidates, starting with the strongest standalone result and testing complementary combinations within the unchanged harness and budget. Reconcile A's ownership changes with B's backtracking and C's replacement buffers; remove superseded work rather than stacking implementations. Verify the complete reference output, including path selection, count and formatting—not just maximum color count. Require semantic justification beyond the four observed correctness cases. Measure the final integrated program directly: gains are not additive, and integration may regress. Retain the best validated measured candidate, favoring simplicity when differences are indistinguishable from noise. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler, target, permissions or budgets, and never introduce test-specific answers.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The supplied source targets Problem A, Maximum Tropical Path. Baseline correctness passed four cases; mean runtime was 8.52 ms, with samples spanning 7.22–10.87 ms. No profile establishes a dominant hotspot. Source-derived opportunities are extensive graph/vector copying, repeated edge scans and color recounting during DFS, and matrix copying/cycle searches inside Prim. Spanning-tree enumeration remains potentially exponential. Prioritize complementary serial improvements on the four-core ARM64 target: these short, variable measurements do not justify assuming threading will help. Reference comparison makes deterministic partition, tree and path ordering important; an equally optimal alternative path may still be rejected.\"\n}"}}
